<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Luba Empire</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Luba</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Luba Empire was a Central African state in the Upemba region of the south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/democratic-republic-of-the-congo">Democratic Republic of the Congo</a>, flourishing from about the sixteenth to the late nineteenth century. It was built around a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sacred-kingship">sacred kingship</a> and a network of tributary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiefdom">chiefdoms</a>, with institutions such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bambudye">Bambudye</a> society preserving its traditions. The Luba model of rule influenced neighbouring states, notably the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lunda-empire">Lunda Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/luba-empire.jpg" alt="Luba Empire" width="320" height="394" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Library of Congress · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lunda_Empire.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its people, the Luyi or Aluyana, later known as the Lozi, probably came from the north, and their own traditions link them to the Lunda and <b>Luba</b> world of the southern Congo basin.</blockquote><a href="/empire/barotseland">Barotseland · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Its formation was closely linked to the influence of the <b>Luba</b>: tradition holds that the Luba hunter-prince Chibinda Ilunga married Lueji (Rweej), heiress of a Lunda ruling line, thereby introducing the Luba model of sacred kingship and its associated institutions.</blockquote><a href="/empire/lunda-empire">Lunda Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Bemba tradition traces the royal Crocodile clan to <b>Luba</b> lands in the Congo basin, from which its founders migrated east and settled on the Lubemba plateau.</blockquote><a href="/state/bemba-kingdom">Bemba Kingdom</a></li><li><blockquote>The Kanyok lived between the Lubilash and Mbuji-Mayi rivers in the southern Kasai and shared many institutions and origin stories with their <b>Luba</b> neighbours.</blockquote><a href="/state/kanyok-kingdom">Kanyok Kingdom</a></li><li><blockquote>According to tradition, the Phiri clan migrated from the <b>Luba</b> lands of the Congo basin and settled south-west of Lake Malawi in the late 15th century.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-maravi">Kingdom of Maravi</a></li></ul></section>
